  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show an area \nof low pressure moving across northwest Minnesota with an \nassociated cold front extending south across the eastern Dakotas. \nWarm advection and shortwave energy ahead of this system are \ngenerating showers across northwest Wisconsin. These showers have \nbeen weakening as they move into high pressure and a dry airmass \nover eastern Wisconsin, where precipitable water values remain \naround 80 percent of normal.\n\nWarm advection ahead of the advancing cold front will continue to\nspread east into the forecast area through tonight. Dry air in \nthe lower levels below 7 kft will delay rain reaching the ground \nacross north-central Wisconsin until late afternoon or early \nevening. The main axis of instability is expected to remain west \nof the region through peak daytime heating. Thunderstorms are \nanticipated to develop in this instability axis later this \nafternoon over Minnesota and western Wisconsin which will move \ninto north- central Wisconsin by mid to late evening while \ngradually weakening. An isolated strong storm could reach central \nWisconsin late in the evening, but severe weather is not \nanticipated tonight as instability wanes. \n\nShower and thunderstorm clusters will continue to move east across\nthe region overnight. On Friday, the cold front will traverse \neastern Wisconsin during peak heating. Following a lull in \nprecipitation late Friday morning to early Friday afternoon, \ndestabilization of 1000 to 1500 J/kg and surface temperatures \napproaching 80 degrees will foster increasing storm coverage after\n3 PM. Modest deep- layer shear of 20 to 25 knots will limit \norganized severe weather, but DCAPE values up to 800 J/kg will \ncreate a localized threat for strong downburst winds in the \nstrongest cells (hail will also be possible). This threat will \ndiminish by 8 to 9 PM over eastern Wisconsin as the front exits.\n\nA brief amplification of an upper-level trough is forecast across\nthe Great Lakes region over the weekend. As a shortwave trough \nmoves through the cyclonic flow on Saturday, there is a chance of showers\nand thunderstorms across north-central and northeast Wisconsin. \nHowever, weak deep-layer shear will prevent significant storm \norganization, keeping the severe weather risk low.\n\nThrough Saturday, most areas will have potential to see at least a\nquarter inch of rainfall (60-80% chance). Scattered amounts\ngreater than 1/2 inch are possible (30-50% chance).\n\nBy Tuesday and Wednesday, another shortwave trough riding along \nthe northern stream is projected to move across the region, \nbringing the next chance for showers and thunderstorms. \nInstability appears modest with this mid-week feature, keeping \nsevere probabilities low. \n\nTemperatures throughout the 7-day period will stay close to late-\nsummer norms, with daily highs mostly ranging from the mid 70s to \nlower 80s and overnight lows in the upper 40s to lower 60s.",
